Building a custom video pipeline using Puppeteer and FFmpeg is the most powerful "bare metal" approach you can take. It gives you complete control over the rendering process without relying on expensive third-party video licenses.

Here is everything you need to understand about how this architecture works, why it is the perfect fit for Kashida, and the technical traps you need to avoid.

The Core Concept
At its simplest, this pipeline treats a web browser as your video rendering engine.

Puppeteer is a Node.js library that allows you to control a "headless" (invisible) Google Chrome browser on your server.

FFmpeg is a command-line tool that is the industry standard for processing, converting, and stitching video and audio files.

Instead of trying to teach a video editor how to render text, you build your news templates as standard web pages (HTML, CSS, JavaScript). You use Puppeteer to open that webpage, take a picture of it 60 times a second, and then hand that massive stack of pictures to FFmpeg to stitch into a final MP4 video.

Why this is mandatory for Kashida
For an Arabic news automation platform, this architecture is not just an option; it is the only reliable way to achieve your core value proposition.

Flawless Arabic Typography: Traditional video rendering engines (like Node Canvas or standard FFmpeg drawtext) break Arabic ligatures, fail to connect letters properly, and struggle with RTL (Right-to-Left) directionality. By using Puppeteer, you are using Google Chrome’s native Blink rendering engine. If your Arabic text and "Kashida" alignments look perfect on a website, they will look perfect in the video.

Web Native Animations: You don't have to learn complex video motion graphics. If you want a news ticker to slide in from the right, you just use standard CSS keyframes or a JavaScript animation library (like GSAP).

Dynamic Data: Injecting a news headline is as simple as manipulating the DOM (e.g., document.getElementById('headline').innerText = "العاجل: ...") before you start recording.

How the Pipeline Works
Here is the exact step-by-step process your backend will execute when a journalist requests a video.

1
Spawn the Browser
Your Node.js server launches a headless instance of Chrome using Puppeteer and sets the viewport exactly to your desired video resolution (e.g., 1920x1080 for landscape, 1080x1920 for TikTok/Reels).

2
Load and Inject Data
Puppeteer loads your local HTML template. Your script injects the specific news data (text, images, journalist names) into the HTML document.

3
Capture the Frames
Puppeteer takes a screenshot of the browser window. It advances the animation by a fraction of a second, takes another screenshot, and repeats this until the animation is finished. These images (usually PNGs) are saved to a temporary folder on your server.

4
Stitch with FFmpeg
Once all screenshots are captured, your Node.js script triggers a command to FFmpeg, telling it to take the folder of PNGs, apply a frame rate (e.g., 30 FPS), merge it with an audio track if needed, and output a compressed .mp4 file.

5
Cleanup
The server deletes the thousands of temporary PNG frames to free up storage space and sends the final MP4 back to the user.

The Biggest Technical Trap: Deterministic Rendering
If you search for "Puppeteer screen recording," you will find tutorials telling you to use real-time screen capture plugins. Do not use them for programmatic video.

If your server CPU spikes for a second while recording a real-time screencast, the browser drops frames, resulting in a laggy, glitchy final video. Instead, you must use Deterministic Rendering (often achieved using Chrome's HeadlessExperimental CDP domain or libraries like puppeteer-capture).

Feature	Real-Time Screen Capture	Deterministic Capture (The Right Way)
How it works	Records the browser live, like a human watching it.	Pauses the browser clock. Takes a photo. Advances clock exactly 16.6ms. Takes photo.
Server Load	High risk. A CPU spike causes dropped frames and stuttering in the video.	Safe. If the server is slow, it just takes longer to generate the video, but the final MP4 is perfectly smooth.
Reproducibility	Varies every time.	Frame-perfect. The exact same HTML will produce the exact same video file, byte for byte.
The Tech Stack to Build This
To wire this together, you will need to install the following on your backend:

puppeteer: To control the browser.

fluent-ffmpeg: A Node.js wrapper that makes it much easier to write FFmpeg commands in JavaScript rather than messy shell strings.

puppeteer-capture (or similar CDP tools): An open-source package specifically designed to handle the "deterministic" frame-by-frame capture mentioned above, ensuring your CSS animations render perfectly without dropped frames.

