First, navigate to a new empty directory in your terminal, open OpenCode, and ask it to set up the environment.

Prompt for OpenCode:

"Set up a new Python backend project for a video rendering engine. I need you to create a virtual environment, activate it, and install the following dependencies: fastapi, uvicorn, playwright, ffmpeg-python, and celery[redis]. After installing, generate a requirements.txt file and initialize a basic project folder structure with directories for api, workers, templates, and temp_frames. Finally, run playwright install chromium."

Step 2: The Arabic HTML/GSAP Template
Next, we need OpenCode to build the webpage that will act as our video canvas. It needs to include GreenSock (GSAP) so we can manually control the animation timeline frame-by-frame.

Prompt for OpenCode:

"Create an HTML file in the templates folder called breaking_news.html. It should include TailwindCSS and GSAP via CDN. Set the body styling to dir="rtl" for Arabic text. Create a standard news lower-third design. Crucially, add a JavaScript script block at the bottom that exposes two functions on the window object: window.loadNewsData(jsonData) which updates the text/colors based on a JSON payload, and window.seekToFrame(frameNumber, fps) which uses GSAP to pause the animation timeline and jump exactly to the calculated time (frameNumber / fps)."

Step 3: The Deterministic Playwright Script
Now, instruct OpenCode to write the exact Python logic that drives the browser. This is the hardest part, so the prompt needs to be specific.

Prompt for OpenCode:

"In the workers directory, create a script called renderer.py. Write an asynchronous Python function using playwright.async_api that opens a headless Chromium browser with a 1080x1920 viewport. It needs to load the breaking_news.html file using a file:// absolute path. Call page.evaluate() to trigger window.loadNewsData() with dummy JSON. Then, create a loop that iterates from frame 0 to 150 (for a 5-second video at 30fps). Inside the loop, evaluate() the window.seekToFrame(i, 30) JS function, and take a deterministic PNG screenshot of the page, saving it to the temp_frames directory sequentially (e.g., frame_0001.png). Make sure it waits for network idle before starting the loop."

Step 4: FFmpeg Stitching and Celery Worker
Finally, wire up the heavy lifting. OpenCode needs to connect the frames to FFmpeg and wrap the whole thing in a background worker.

Prompt for OpenCode:

"Update the renderer.py script. After the Playwright loop finishes and the browser closes, use ffmpeg-python to read the sequential PNGs in the temp_frames directory and compile them into an MP4 video at 30fps using the libx264 codec. Save it as output.mp4. Once the MP4 is generated, delete all the PNG files to clean up. Finally, wrap this entire execution flow into a Celery task connected to a local Redis instance."

